I have two children and am part of the Crawl devteam. To me, that's 
enough grip on reality, I don't need more.

For anyone who is still reading (I will not post in this thread any 
further): You can acquire responsibilty even if you are not formally 
obliged to anything. Children are a good example: technically, it suffices 
to not beat them and feed them until they're grown up. In reality, the 
responsibility is everywhere.

The Nethack devteam has created a program that many users enjoy. This is 
good. Since there is no money involved, no one is entitled to demand 
anything from the devteam. Still, they have a responsibility for their 
project, and they fulfill it partially: their collecting of bugs and 
fixing them is one instance of this.

Some may be completely blind to this fact and it can't be nailed down in 
formal terms, of course. So let me close by depicting a worst-case 
scenario that would show a true lack of responsibilities and its 
consequences (I hope this is fiction!): Suppose that the devteam tried to 
write Nethack from the ground up in a new, better language, say Java. 
Suppose furthermore that they got inevitably stuck and can go neither 
forward nor backward anymore, for four years, say. Additionally suppose 
that a group of enthusiastic r.g.r.n collaborateurs forms a team with the 
goal to rewrite Nethack in a new, better language like Javak. Five years 
from now they are left with no working code (another assumption). The 
Nethack devteam having silently watched (final assumption that they 
watch) ... shows lack of responsibility to me.

